README-FORTRAN -- the RTF/68K Fortran-77 system===============================================This disk carries RTF/68K, a Fortran-77 compiler, with its drivers andrun-time library.  Load the library first:  load /dd/CMDS/os9lib`os9lib' is the run-time library, not a program.  Every program belowlinks it by name; without it each exits at once, printing nothing, withstatus 221 (E_MNF).  `load' is your own OS-9's, which SYS/login puts onPATH from /h1/CMDS.  rtf       the compiler: " command must be: RTF pathname [options..] "  for       " command: for file{,file} [-rtfopt{,rtfopt}] "  lnk       " command: lnk file{,file} [-l68opts] "  lnk.org   the same as lnk, with start-up code named fstart.r  biory     prompts, in German, for a name and a birth date  creadoc   collects the documentation header of every .f file in the            current directory into creadoc.txt.Compiling, assembling, linking------------------------------rtf emits 68000 assembly.  Your own OS-9's r68 and l68 finish the job;SYS/login makes r68, l68, del and the shell resident from /h1.  Thewhole chain, on SRC/rtf/fact.f:    load /dd/CMDS/os9lib    rtf fact.f                       compiles to fact.a    r68 -q fact.a -o=fact.r    r68 -q rtfstart.a -o=rtfstart.r  start-up code, SRC/rtf/rtfstart.a    l68 rtfstart.r fact.r -l=sys.l -o=fact    fact                             prints 1! to 12!r68 reads `oskdefs.d' from the directory it runs in, and sys.l is yourown library: copy both beside the source first.  l68 writes the programto your execution directory.The drivers do the same with less typing.  `for fact' runs rtf, r68 anddel.  `lnk fact' links fact.r, taking sys.l and the start-up code from/h0/LIB and writing the program to /h0/CMDS.  rtfstart.r andrtfstart020.r are in LIB already, so `lnk' needs only your sys.l copiedbeside them (and `lnk.org' a copy named fstart.r).The as0, as1, as4, as5 and as11 assemblers on this disk are 6800, 6801,6804, 6805 and 68HC11 cross-assemblers, not 68k.What is here------------  DOC/rtf/rtfman.txt    the RTF/68K manual, version 2.13, 12 March 1987:                        compiler and run-time library  DOC/rtf/biory.doc     creadoc's output for biory.f and creadoc.f  DOC/rtf/creadoc.doc  SRC/rtf/*.f           biory, creadoc, and demos: prime, bench, float,                        div, vtest, fact  SRC/rtf/rtfstart.a    the start-up code, and its 68020 variant  LIB/rtfstart.r        the same, assembled, as the RTF archive  LIB/rtfstart020.r     shipped themRTF/68K is a CERN product; its manual says the package is distributedfree.  It came to this collection from EFFO public-domain disk 1 andEFFO forum disk 5.